{
  "moneyMethods": [
    "deposit (#[payable]): money IN — caller attaches NEAR; contract credits env::attached_deposit() to the caller's recorded balance in `balances`; rejected when deposits_paused is true or the attachment is zero; the attached NEAR simply stays on the contract account.",
    "withdraw: money OUT — caller passes amount and receiver_id; contract checks the caller's recorded balance, deducts the amount with saturating_sub, then Promise::new(receiver_id).transfer(amount); not marked #[payable], so stray attachments panic; the ledger deduction and the actual transfer happen in separate receipts, so a failed transfer promise would leave the deduction committed (only realistically possible if the contract account itself is short of NEAR).",
    "balance_of (view): no money moves — returns the recorded NearToken balance for a given account, defaulting to 0.",
    "new (#[init]): no money moves — constructs the Vault with a caller-chosen owner, deposits_paused=false, and an empty balances map; runs once on an uninitialized account."
  ],
  "balanceStorage": [
    "balances (LookupMap<AccountId, NearToken>, storage prefix b\"b\"): the only ledger — per-account credited NEAR from deposits; withdrawals are authorized solely against this record, and the contract implicitly assumes the account's real NEAR balance covers the sum of recorded balances (direct transfers or refunds to the contract account are not captured here).",
    "deposits_paused (bool): control flag, not a balance — when true, deposit() refuses attached NEAR.",
    "owner (AccountId): control field, not a balance — the sole identity set_deposits_paused checks; no in-code way to rotate it."
  ],
  "privilegedMethods": [
    {
      "method": "set_deposits_paused",
      "whoMayCall": "only the AccountId stored in `owner` (fixed at init); can block new deposits but cannot touch balances or move funds"
    },
    {
      "method": "new (#[init])",
      "whoMayCall": "any caller, exactly once, on a fresh/uninitialized account — whoever initializes picks the owner, so init timing and deploy authority matter"
    }
  ],
  "standingNote": "In-code privilege is limited to the owner's deposit pause; no method here can move another user's funds or edit the ledger. However, the source contains no self-upgrade, no owner rotation, and no code-change guard, so true control lives off-chain: key and upgrade control has to be checked on the deployed account — its access keys (any full-access key can redeploy code, wiping or replacing `balances`, or drain the account's real NEAR regardless of the ledger), multisig settings, and who can call #[init] first. Also verify on-chain that the contract account's actual NEAR balance is at least the sum of recorded balances, since the ledger and the real balance can diverge via direct transfers, refunds, or a failed withdraw transfer promise after the deduction commits."
}

